Analysis Method / packages Seasonality &amp; calendar effects STL, month/weekday effects, working-day adjustment Forecast with honest validation <code>fable</code> (<code>ETS</code>, <code>ARIMA</code>, <code>SNAIVE</code> benchmark) with rolling-origin CV (<code>tsibble::stretch_tsibble</code>) Anomaly detection STL residual z-scores / IQR on residuals; flag, don't auto-delete Mix analysis Share of channel / type over time (compositional plots; log-ratio if you model shares) Elasticity &amp; growth Log-difference growth, CAGR with bootstrap interval <p>Reusable backtest scaffold (pure function, unit-testable):</p> <pre><code>#' Rolling-origin backtest of ETS / ARIMA against a seasonal-naive benchmark
#' @param df data frame with one row per month
#' @param date_col,value_col column names (strings)
#' @export
backtest_forecast &lt;- function(df, date_col, value_col, h = 3, initial = 24, step = 1) {
  ts &lt;- df |&gt;
    dplyr::transmute(month = tsibble::yearmonth(.data[[date_col]]),
                     y     = .data[[value_col]]) |&gt;
    tsibble::as_tsibble(index = month)

  if (any(tsibble::has_gaps(ts)$.gaps)) {
    stop("Monthly series has gaps; fix upstream or handle explicitly.", call. = FALSE)
  }
  if (nrow(ts) &lt; initial + h) stop("Series too short for requested backtest.", call. = FALSE)

  ts |&gt;
    tsibble::stretch_tsibble(.init = initial, .step = step) |&gt;
    fabletools::model(
      snaive = fable::SNAIVE(y),
      ets    = fable::ETS(y),
      arima  = fable::ARIMA(y)
    ) |&gt;
    fabletools::forecast(h = h) |&gt;
    fabletools::accuracy(ts)
}
</code></pre> <p>Rule: a forecast model is only reported if it beats <code>SNAIVE</code> on the backtest. Report prediction intervals, not just point forecasts.</p>

Use Font Notes Body, tables, headings Inter (Regular, Italic, SemiBold, SemiBold Italic) tabular numerals on, so digit columns align Identifiers, SQL, ids JetBrains Mono (Regular, Bold) table names, run ids, error categories Cover title only Inter SemiBold, large no second display font <p>Both families are open licence. The <code>.ttf</code> files and their licence texts are in <code>reports/fonts/</code> and committed, because CI and the Airflow image have no network to fetch fonts. If a file is missing the build must still succeed:</p> <pre><code>% Font setup with a fallback so a missing file never breaks the build.
\usepackage{fontspec}
\IfFileExists{fonts/Inter-Regular.ttf}{
  \setmainfont{Inter-Regular.ttf}[Path=fonts/, BoldFont=Inter-SemiBold.ttf,
    ItalicFont=Inter-Italic.ttf, BoldItalicFont=Inter-SemiBoldItalic.ttf,
    Numbers=Monospaced]
}{
  \setmainfont{TeX Gyre Heros}
}
</code></pre> <p>Do the same for the mono font with <code>TeX Gyre Cursor</code> as the fallback. Never ship a font without its licence. If the font files are absent, ask a human to add them; do not invent or download them from an unknown source.</p> <p>Current state. The font files are in the repository: Inter 4.1 and JetBrains Mono 2.304 (SIL OFL 1.1), each next to its licence text in <code>reports/fonts/</code>. The style file loads them with <code>fontspec</code> and <code>reports/latexmkrc</code> sets <code>$pdf_mode = 5</code> (XeLaTeX) for that. A pdfLaTeX build (an old checkout, or a manual <code>pdflatex main.tex</code>) keeps the scalable Latin Modern family (<code>\usepackage{lmodern}</code>), which <code>microtype</code> needs to expand. Latin Modern is metric compatible with the layout, so the fallback changes the glyphs, not the page structure. The charts register the same files with matplotlib, so the document and the figures share one typeface. Provenance and the update procedure: <code>reports/fonts/README.md</code>.</p> <p>Scale: base 10.5 pt, line spacing 1.2, section titles 16 pt, subsection 12 pt, table text 9 pt, captions 8.5 pt. A4, margins 20 mm left and right, 22 mm top and bottom, text width about 170 mm.</p>
